            </svg>
            Buying Tip
          </p>
          <p class="text-amber-800 text-sm leading-relaxed">
            Because ProForm and NordicTrack share a parent company, their
            sales calendars tend to overlap. If you're deciding between the
            Pro 2000 and the Commercial 1750, watch both prices during the
            same promotional window — sometimes the price gap between them
            narrows enough during a sale that the Commercial 1750 becomes
            the clearer buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p>
          It's also worth being clear-eyed about what "lighter-duty" means
          in practice, since it's easy to read that phrase as a bigger red
          flag than it actually is. ProForm doesn't build the Pro 2000 with
          budget-bin components — it's still a 300-pound-capacity treadmill
          with a real steel frame, a genuine 3.6 CHP motor, and the same
          10-year frame warranty as the Commercial 1750. "Lighter-duty"
          relative to NordicTrack's Commercial tier means it's engineered
          and tested against a somewhat lower expected duty cycle, not that
          it's fragile. For the overwhelming majority of home users, who
          are nowhere near the duty cycle either machine is stress-tested
          against, that distinction ends up mattering far less in real life
          than it does on a spec comparison chart.
        </p>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Who Should Skip This Treadmill</h2>
        <p>
          The Pro 2000 is a strong value, but it isn't the right machine
          for every shopper who lands on this page. If you run daily and
          expect to put serious mileage on the machine for years, the extra
          durability margin of the NordicTrack Commercial 1750 is worth the
          roughly $200 premium — that's exactly the kind of use case where
          the lighter-duty motor and frame are most likely to show their
          limits sooner. If you live in an apartment and need the treadmill
          to genuinely disappear between uses, its large folded footprint
          will frustrate you more than it helps; a dedicated compact
          folding treadmill from our
          <a href="/best-folding-treadmill-reviews/">folding treadmill guide</a>
          will serve you better. And if you know for certain you'll never
          pay for an iFit subscription, you're paying for touchscreen
          hardware you'll rarely use at full value — a simpler
          console-based machine, or ProForm's own Power 995i, may be the
          smarter buy from our
          <a href="/best-treadmill-under-1000-reviews/">budget treadmill guide</a>.
        </p>
